<<~ ahu #/head>>

! The Burned Library

The ''Floating Librarians of Mu'' have spent nearly five hundred years recording the lost and
wild magics into ''a new grammar'' — one attested working at a time, patient as coral, from the
High Delos Archive and the Library's own drifting stacks.

Then the ''Dream-zone Library of Alexander caught fire during the time-glitch event'' — within
the last hundred years, and ''the exact date stands lost to the time-glitch itself'': every
record that would fix the day rides locked or contradicts its neighbors, which the Bureau of
Chronometry (Remnant) names ''their oldest cold case''. Because //when// itself
ran unreliable, the fire did what no honest fire can: ''it spread through l-space instead of
shelf-space, and LOCKED much of the records.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-lock>>

!! What a Locked Record Reads Like

Not destroyed — which would at least read clean. ''Locked'': sealed workings mid-sentence,
grammar with the verbs fused shut, texts that read until the third line and then read //back//.
A locked record still knows its own name; none of them answer a stranger's hand. The lock-fire
burns inward, consuming nothing — a library holding its breath inside the burn.

//(This canon grounds the Locked Powers mechanic: any attribute of a power may ride locked,
encrypted, or damaged; unlock vectors run through salvage, noosphere permissions, corruption
treatment, and factional sponsorship — every one of them a key someone cut after the fire.)//

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-kuleana>>

!! The Kahu Kuleana

''You cannot copy a burned book.'' The fire rides the copies. What survives clean are the
''pattern integrities'' — the shapes the fallen and still-living nakama Librarians attested, the
wave under the locked water. So the restoration runs the only way it can: ''witness kept frozen
and literal beneath, the pattern re-stood fresh above, never letting the derivation touch the
burn.'' Room by room, through the ''Arcana virtual-rooms'' the Archive's floor plan gives —
thirteen of them, the warding-room first, because you learn the counter-grammar before you open
doors in a burned library.

The kahu — keepers holding Library caps across many cabals — carry this work. The restored shelf
runs small and growing; the drift stays where the drift was attested (a slipped scribal hand
survives as canon — //Tragic Missile// — because the slip carries attestation and the correction
would carry only invention). The well of the unshelved holds everything carried out of the fire
in satchels and dying breaths: patterns without rooms yet, waiting for someone to give each one
its room back.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/voss-interfaces>>

!! The Voss-Interfaces

Query the Archive's deep stacks often enough and a ''temporary user interface spins up'' — index
cards and lamplight in the suggestion of robes, a hexagram where a face would go, no canonical
face at all. They advise in letterforms: //keep your syntax loose, your metaphors polite, and
your exits memorized.// Some information they hold stays locked — not by the fire; by politeness.

<<~/ahu>>

